*Henosis* ([[/wiki/Ancient_Greek_language][Ancient Greek]]:
[[https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%E1%BC%95%CE%BD%CF%89%CF%83%CE%B9%CF%82][ἕνωσις]])
is the classical Greek word for mystical "oneness", "union" or "unity".
In [[/wiki/Neoplatonism][Neoplatonism]], henosis is unification with
what is fundamental in reality: the One
([[https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%CE%B5%E1%BC%B7%CF%82][Τὸ Ἕν]]), the
Source, or
[[/wiki/Monad_(philosophy)][Monad]].^{[[#cite_note-FOOTNOTEStamatellos200737-1][[1]]]}
The Neoplatonic concept has precedents in the Greek
[[/wiki/Mystery_religions][mystery
religions]]^{[[#cite_note-FOOTNOTEAngus197552-2][[2]]]} as well as
parallels in [[/wiki/Eastern_philosophy][Eastern
philosophy]].^{[[#cite_note-FOOTNOTEGregorios2002-3][[3]]]} It is
further developed in the [[/wiki/Corpus_Hermeticum][Corpus Hermeticum]],
in [[/wiki/Christian_theology][Christian theology]],
[[/wiki/Islamic_Mysticism][Islamic Mysticism]],
[[/wiki/Soteriology][soteriology]] and [[/wiki/Mysticism][mysticism]],
and is an important factor in the historical development of
[[/wiki/Monotheism][monotheism]] during [[/wiki/Late_Antiquity][Late
Antiquity]].

** Etymology
:PROPERTIES:
:CUSTOM_ID: Etymology
:END:
[[[/w/index.php?title=Henosis&action=edit&section=1][edit]]]

The term is relatively common in classical texts, and has the meaning of
"union" or "unity".^{[[#cite_note-5][[note 1]]]}

** Process of unification
:PROPERTIES:
:CUSTOM_ID: Process_of_unification
:END:
[[[/w/index.php?title=Henosis&action=edit&section=2][edit]]]

See also: [[/wiki/Nepsis][Nepsis]], [[/wiki/Theoria][Theoria]], and
[[/wiki/Theosis_(Eastern_Christian_theology)][Theosis (Eastern Christian
theology)]]

Henosis, or primordial unity, is rational and deterministic, emanating
from [[/wiki/Indeterminism][indeterminism]] an uncaused cause. Each
individual as a [[/wiki/Macrocosm_and_microcosm][microcosm]] reflects
the gradual ordering of the universe referred to as the
[[/wiki/Macrocosm_and_microcosm][macrocosm]]. In mimicking the demiurge
([[/wiki/Nous][divine mind]]), one unites with The One or Monad. Thus
the process of unification, of "The Being" and "The One", is called
henosis, the culmination of which is
[[/wiki/Apotheosis][deification]].^{[/[[/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed][citation
needed]]/]}

** Plotinus
:PROPERTIES:
:CUSTOM_ID: Plotinus
:END:
[[[/w/index.php?title=Henosis&action=edit&section=3][edit]]]

Main article: [[/wiki/Plotinus][Plotinus]]

Henosis for Plotinus (204/5--270 CE) was defined in his works as a
reversing of the ontological process of consciousness via
[[/wiki/Meditation][meditation]] (or
[[/wiki/Contemplation][contemplation]]) toward no thought
([[/wiki/Nous][nous]] or [[/wiki/Demiurge][demiurge]]) and no division
([[/wiki/Dyad_(symbol)][dyad]]) within the individual (being). As is
specified in the writings of [[/wiki/Plotinus][Plotinus]] on
[[/wiki/Henology][Henology]],^{[[#cite_note-6][[note 2]]]} one can reach
a [[/wiki/Tabula_rasa][tabula rasa]], a blank state where the individual
may grasp or merge with The One. This absolute simplicity means that the
nous or the person is then dissolved, completely absorbed back into the
Monad.

Within the /[[/wiki/Enneads][Enneads]]/ of Plotinus, the Monad can be
referred to as the Good above the
demiurge.^{[[#cite_note-books.google.com-7][[5]]][[#cite_note-8][[6]]]}
The Monad or [[/wiki/Dunamis][dynamis]] (force) is of one singular
expression (the will or the one is the good), all is contained in the
Monad and the Monad is all and in all
([[/wiki/Panentheism][panentheism]]). All division is reconciled in the
one, the final stage before reaching singularity, and what is called
duality (dyad) is completely reconciled in the Monad, Source or One (see
[[/wiki/Monism][monism]]). As the source or substance of all things, the
Monad is all encompassing. As infinite and indeterminate, all is
reconciled in the dynamis or one. It is the demiurge or second emanation
that is the nous in Plotinus. It is the demiurge (creator, action,
energy) or nous that "perceives," and therefore causes the force
(potential or One) to manifest as energy, or the
[[/wiki/Dyad_(Greek_philosophy)][dyad]], called the material world. Nous
as being, being and perception (intellect) manifest what is called soul
([[/wiki/Anima_mundi][World
Soul]]).^{[[#cite_note-books.google.com-7][[5]]]}

Plotinus words his teachings to reconcile not only Plato with Aristotle,
but also various world religions that he had personal contact with
during his various travels. Plotinus' works have an
[[/wiki/Ascetic][ascetic]] character in that they reject matter as an
illusion (non-existent). Matter was strictly treated as
[[/wiki/Immanent][immanent]], with matter as essential to its being,
having no true or [[/wiki/Transcendence_(philosophy)][transcendental]]
character or essence, substance or [[/wiki/Ousia][ousia]]. This approach
is called philosophical
[[/wiki/Idealism][Idealism]].^{[[#cite_note-10][[note 3]]]}

*** Phases
:PROPERTIES:
:CUSTOM_ID: Phases
:END:
[[[/w/index.php?title=Henosis&action=edit&section=4][edit]]]

Plotinus' phases of "mystical union with the One" as given by
[[/wiki/Alexander_J._Mazur][Mazur]]
(2021):^{[[#cite_note-Mazur-11][[8]]]}

- Phase 1, [[/wiki/Catharsis][Catharsis]]: self-purification
  (/aphairesis/) from any contamination with multiplicity (of any
  thought, knowledge, or mental activity); "removing" Being itself
  (/Enneads/ III.8.10)
- Phase 2, Mystical self-reversion: "The intellect ... must ‘withdraw
  backwards' and surrender itself to what lies behind it" (/Enneads/
  III.8.9)
- Phase 3, [[/wiki/Self-realization][Autophany]]: luminous vision of
  one's own self
  - Phase 3.2, Self-unification: to "become one from many" (/Enneads/
    VI.9.3)
- Phase 4, [[/wiki/Ego_death][Annihilation]]: discussed in the /Enneads/
  VI.9
- Phase 5, Union with the One
  - Phase 5.2, Desubjectification

Passages in the /[[/wiki/Enneads][Enneads]]/ describing the different
stages of mystical union with the One can be found in I.6, IV.8, VI.9,
III.8, V.3, V.5, V.8, and VI.7-8.^{[[#cite_note-Mazur-11][[8]]]}

** Iamblichus of Chalcis
:PROPERTIES:
:CUSTOM_ID: Iamblichus_of_Chalcis
:END:
[[[/w/index.php?title=Henosis&action=edit&section=5][edit]]]

See also: [[/wiki/Greek_hero_cult][Greek hero cult]]

Within the works of [[/wiki/Iamblichus_of_Chalcis][Iamblichus of
Chalcis]] (c. 245 -- c. 325 AD), the process of achieving
henosis---union with the divine---is not accomplished through
contemplation alone, as in the teachings of
[[/wiki/Plotinus][Plotinus]], but through the ritual practice of
[[/wiki/Theurgy][theurgy]]. By reenacting the creative ordering of the
cosmos, [[/wiki/Initiates][initiates]] mimic the actions of the
[[/wiki/Demiurge][demiurge]] and align themselves with the
[[/wiki/Divine_order][divine order]]. These rituals, drawn in part from
the [[/wiki/Mystery_religions][mystery religions]], serve to unite the
inner and outer aspects of the [[/wiki/Self][self]], restoring harmony
and opening the way to divine union. Central to this process is the
assumption of divine forms---a kind of [[/wiki/Divine_embodiment][divine
embodiment]]---through which the practitioner ritually identifies with
higher beings or
intelligences.^{[[#cite_note-FOOTNOTEShawMilbank2014-12][[9]]]} Through
these embodied enactments, the soul ascends the hierarchy of being and
is gradually reintegrated into the divine source, culminating in
henosis.

